2026-06-30 15:12:45,049 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-eac71009-5b05-45dc-a76d-387aa6f21089', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: disease\nsdrf_column: characteristics[disease]\nontology: EFO:0000408, MONDO, DOID, PATO\ndescription: Disease state of the sample.\ninstructions: For healthy/normal samples use "normal" (PATO:0000461). For diseased\n  samples use MONDO or DOID terms. Return null only if completely unstated.\n  Most XL-MS samples are "normal" unless explicitly studying a disease model.\n\nterm: phenotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phenotype]\nontology: EFO:0000651, PATO, EFO\ndescription: Observable characteristics or traits (drug sensitivity, molecular markers,\n  expression phenotypes, environmental responses).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. This captures how the sample behaves/appears\n  (distinct from disease diagnosis).\n\nterm: treatment\nsdrf_column: characteristics[treatment]\nontology: EFO:0000727, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Treatment or perturbation applied to the sample (drug, stimulus,\n  environmental stress, growth condition change).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable. Examples: "doxycycline\n  treatment", "heat shock", "DMSO vehicle control".\n\nterm: compound\nsdrf_column: characteristics[compound]\nontology: CHEBI:24431, CHEBI, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Chemical compound or drug applied to the sample.\ninstructions: Use ChEBI if possible. Return null if no compound treatment. Do NOT\n  include the crosslinking reagent here — that goes in comment[cross-linker].\n\nterm: dose\nsdrf_column: characteristics[dose]\nontology: NCIT:C25488\ndescription: Dose or concentration of compound treatment.\npattern: number + unit (e.g. 10 mg/kg, 50 uM, 100 nM, 10 mg/mL)\ninstructions: Return null if no compound treatment or dose not stated.\n\nterm: genetic modification\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genetic modification]\nontology: EFO:0000510, EFO\ndescription: Method of genetic modification (knockout, knockdown, overexpression,\n  transduction). Describes HOW the modification was done.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: perturbation\nsdrf_column: characteristics[perturbation]\nontology: PRIDE:0000700\ndescription: Type of perturbation applied (environmental/experimental perturbation).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: depletion\nsdrf_column: characteristics[depletion]\nontology: PRIDE:0000020\ndescription: Whether abundant protein depletion was performed.\nallowed_values: no depletion, depletion, not applicable\ninstructions: Return "no depletion" if not mentioned. Return "depletion" if\n  high-abundance protein removal is described (e.g. albumin depletion).\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nCross-linking mass spectrometry uncovers protein interactions and functional assemblies in synaptic vesicle membranes\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nSynaptic vesicles are storage organelles for neurotransmitters in the pre-synaptic cytosol. When an action potential arrives at the pre-synaptic terminal, they fuse with the pre-synaptic membrane and neurotransmitters are released. In this project, we set out to study the protein interactions formed in synaptic vesicle membranes. For this, we first assessed the proteome of five independent vesicle preparations. Using chemical cross-linking, we then tackled the interactions of the major protein components. To gain further insights, we combined this approach with a chemical labelling strategy. Specific protein interactions were then studied after (i) treating the vesicles with Botulinum neurotoxin B, (ii) binding the soluble SNARE complex, and (iii) fusion of the vesicles with empty liposomes. Our findings reveal stable interaction modules in synaptic vesicles.\n\n[METHODS]\nSynaptic vesicles were purified from rat brain as previously described (Huttner, et al., 1983; Nagy, et al., 1976). The typical protein concentration of purified synaptic vesicles was 2 \uf06dg/\uf06dl. The proteins of synaptic vesicles were separated by gel electrophoresis. Gel electrophoresis was performed using the NuPAGE system (Thermo Fisher Scientific) according to the manufacturer’s protocols. Protein bands were stained with InstantBlue Coomassie Protein Stain solution (Expedeon).  Entire gel lanes were cut into 23 equally sized gel slices. The proteins were then hydrolysed with trypsin as described previously (Shevchenko, et al., 2006). For this, the proteins were first reduced with dithiothreitol and reduced cysteine residues were then alkylated with iodoacetamide. The proteins were hydrolysed with trypsin at 37°C overnight. Extracted peptides were dried in a vacuum centrifuge and dissolved in 2 % (v/v) acetonitrile/ 0.1 % (v/v) formic acid for LC-MS/MS analysis. For chemical cross-linking of synaptic vesicle proteins, synaptic vesicles (protein concentration 2 \uf06dg/\uf06dg) were cross-linked with 10 mM BS3 for 1h at 25°C and 300 rpm in a thermomixer. The proteins were then precipitated with 1 vol. ice-cold ethanol and 1/10 vol. 3M sodium acetate, pH 5.3 at -20°C. The protein pellet was washed with 80 % (v/v) ice-cold ethanol and dried in a vacuum centrifuge.  For tryptic hydrolysis, the protein pellet was dissolved in 20 \uf06dl 1 % (m/v) RapiGest (Waters) in 25 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8.5. Proteins were then reduced with 20 \uf06dl 50 mM dithiothreitol in 25 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8.5 for 1h at 37°C. Reduced cysteines were alkylated with 20 \uf06dl 100 mM iodoacetamide in 25 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8.5 for 1h at 37°C in the dark. For tryptic digestion, the concentration of RapiGest was diluted to 0.1 % (m/v) with 25 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8.5. Trypsin (Promega) was added at a 1:20 enzyme:protein ratio followed by incubation at 37°C overnight. RapiGest was then degraded by addition of 40 \uf06dl of 5 % (v/v) trifluoric acid followed by incubation for 2h at 37°C. Degraded RapiGest was removed by centrifugation and tryptic peptides were dried in a vacuum centrifuge. Cross-linked di-peptides were enriched by peptide size-exclusion chromatography on an SuperdexTM peptide 3.2/300 column using an ÄKTA pure chromatography system (GE Healthcare). For this, the peptides were dissolved in 60 % (v/v) acetonitrile, 0.1 % (v/v) trifluoric acid followed by isocratic separation at a flow rate of 50 \uf06dl/min. Elution of the peptides was monitored at 280 nm. Fractions of 50 \uf06dl were collected. Fractions containing cross-linked di-peptides as well as linear peptides were dried in a vacuum centrifuge and dissolved in 2 % (v/v) acetonitrile/ 0.1 % (v/v) formic acid for LC-MS/MS analysis. For labelling with sulfo-NHS-acetate, a total protein amount of approx. 30 \uf06dg was incubated with 5 and 10 mM sulfo-NHS-acetate for 15 min at 23 °C. The proteins were then precipitated and digested with RapiGest and prepared for LC-MS/MS analysis as described for cross-linked proteins.  For labelling with DEPC, a total protein amount of approx. 30 \uf06dg was incubated with 5 and 10 mM DEPC for 10 min at 37 °C. The proteins were then precipitated as described above. The protein pellet was dissolved in 8 M urea in 25 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.9 followed by incubation for 15 min at room-temperature. The proteins were reduced with 5 mM dithiothreitol for 30 min at 23°C followed by alkylation with 20 mM iodoacetamide for 30 min at 23°C. The solution was then diluted with 100 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8.5 to a final concentration of 2 M urea. Tryptic hydrolysis was performed at 37 °C overnight (enzyme:protein 1:20). Generated peptides were desalted using ZipTips (Merck).\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
